[stderr] ## Domain vocabulary and state ownership
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Term | Meaning / owner |
[stderr] | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Campaign | The fixed first-release sequence from new game through ending; owns completion state. |
[stderr] | Scene | One authored playable location: town or dungeon; owns exits, encounter zones, and presentation references. |
[stderr] | Quest | Authored objective and state machine that gates campaign progress and rewards. |
[stderr] | Party | Player-controlled combat group; owns members' current combat-ready values and progression. Exact party size is an authoring decision. |
[stderr] | Encounter | A combat instance created from an authored encounter definition; never persists mid-turn. |
[stderr] | Item | Authored definition; an inventory stack/reference is the player-owned instance/count. |
[stderr] | Save snapshot | Versioned, validated, atomic representation of durable player state at a safe boundary. |
[stderr] | Checkpoint | Authored or system-selected safe snapshot point; it is not a promise to restore arbitrary moment-to-moment state. |
[stderr] | Content package | Versioned original scenes, dialogue, quests, encounters, items, translations, and audiovisual assets delivered together. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## System relationship map
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```text
[stderr] Content package ──validates/defines──> Scene ──starts──> Encounter
[stderr]       │                                  │                  │
[stderr]       ├──defines──> Dialogue ──updates──> Quest <──rewards───┘
[stderr]       │                                  │       │
[stderr]       └──defines──> Items ──held by──> Inventory │
[stderr]                                                  │
[stderr] Input + accessibility ──drives──> Game shell ──> exploration / UI
[stderr]                                                  │
[stderr] Quest + inventory + party + scene ──snapshot──> Save / checkpoint
[stderr] Save version <──compatible-with── Content package / PWA release
[stderr] PWA cache + release workflow ──protect──> runnable assets and save boundary
[stderr] Error recovery + minimal diagnostics ──observe──> shell, persistence, cache
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] **Cross-domain invariants and lifecycle boundaries**
[stderr] 
[stderr] - A quest transition, reward grant, and save snapshot are ordered: validate
[stderr]   transition, apply reward once, then checkpoint; retrying cannot duplicate a
[stderr]   reward.
[stderr] - An encounter may read party, item, and quest state but changes durable state
[stderr]   only after a resolved turn/encounter safe boundary. A crash cannot restore a
[stderr]   half-consumed item or half-applied reward.
[stderr] - A scene exit is available only when its authored quest predicate is true.
[stderr]   Content validation ensures every required predicate, target, reward, and
[stderr]   localisation key exists.
[stderr] - Settings affect presentation/input immediately but never campaign rules.
[stderr]   The same semantic action must be available through supported keyboard,
[stderr]   mouse, touch, and gamepad adaptations.
[stderr] - Save snapshots record content/schema version and are written atomically.
[stderr]   Cache replacement and application migration happen only outside combat and
[stderr]   only while a valid fallback save remains intact.
[stderr] - The local offline campaign neither needs identity nor sends player state.
[stderr]   Any later account is an optional replication boundary, not campaign truth.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Accepted domain chapters
[stderr] 
[stderr] ### 1. Game shell, session, input, and responsive presentation
[stderr] 
[stderr] **Entities/lifecycle:** `Session` moves `launching → title → new-or-resume →
[stderr] playing ↔ paused → ending → title`; `InputAction` is mapped per supported input
[stderr] method; `ViewportProfile` selects responsive layout. Authoring inputs are title
[stderr] copy, control hints, menu routes, and UI layouts. Rules: pause blocks world and
[stderr] combat advancement; focus loss pauses or reaches a safe state; UI action labels
[stderr] remain readable and semantic across screen sizes. Dependencies are accessibility,
[stderr] rendering, persistence, and content. Outcome is an understandable, controllable
[stderr] start and return path. Edge cases are no existing save, focus loss, rotation,
[stderr] unavailable gamepad, unsupported browser feature, and input remap conflict.
[stderr] **First-release boundary:** web/PWA keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad
[stderr] adaptation; one shared game core. No native-client shell or arbitrary controller
[stderr] configuration guarantee.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ### 2. Exploration, town, dungeon, and campaign content
[stderr] 
[stderr] **Entities/lifecycle:** `Scene` is `unloaded → entered → active → exited`;
[stderr] `Exit` is `locked | available | used`; `Interactable` is `available → used` or
[stderr] reusable as authored. Inputs are original tile/layout data, collision shapes,
[stderr] spawn points, NPCs, exits, encounter zones, dialogue, and asset references.
[stderr] Rules: exactly one town and one dungeon form the first campaign; collision and
[stderr] gates must leave a completable route. Dependencies are narrative, quests,
[stderr] combat, rendering, and saves. Outcome is readable navigation to the ending.
[stderr] Edge cases: blocked exit, empty interaction, absent optional item, and invalid
[stderr] content reference. **Boundary:** no extra regions, procedural worlds, or shared
[stderr] levels.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ### 3. Narrative, quests, onboarding, and ending
[stderr] 
[stderr] **Entities/lifecycle:** `Dialogue` is `available → shown → acknowledged`;
[stderr] `Quest` is `unavailable → available → accepted → objective-complete →
[stderr] claimed → completed` (or `failed` only where authored); `TutorialStep` is
[stderr] `pending → presented → understood`; `Campaign` is `not-started → active →
[stderr] completed`. Inputs are original characters, text, localisation keys, objective
[stderr] predicates, rewards, and ending sequence. Rules: tutorial teaches a battle and
[stderr] saving before unrestricted progression; required quests cannot be abandoned;
[stderr] completion is idempotent. Dependencies are scenes, combat, inventory, UI, and
[stderr] saves. Outcomes are clear objectives and a short finish. Edge cases: repeated
[stderr] NPC talk, dialogue after completion, missing translation, and an invalid quest
[stderr] predicate. **Boundary:** one authored story path; no branching campaign,
[stderr] seasonal narrative, or user-authored stories.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ### 4. Party combat, encounters, rewards, and balance
[stderr] 
[stderr] **Entities/lifecycle:** `Encounter` is `pending → active → player-turn →
[stderr] resolution → won | lost | escaped-if-authored`; `Combatant` is `ready → acting
[stderr] → resolved → defeated`; `Reward` is `pending → granted`. Inputs are party and
[stderr] enemy definitions, actions, targeting, encounter placement, reward tables, and
[stderr] balance values. Rules: only legal actions/targets resolve; turn order is visible;
[stderr] reward grants once; defeat returns to a safe checkpoint. Dependencies are party,
[stderr] inventory, quest transitions, audiovisual feedback, and saves. Outcome is a
[stderr] learnable turn-based first battle and reliable progress. Edge cases: no legal
[stderr] target, empty item list, invalid action request, pause/focus loss, crash during
[stderr] resolution, and stale UI selection. **Boundary:** authored encounters only; no
[stderr] multiplayer, ranked modes, live balance changes, or mid-combat save guarantee.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ### 5. Inventory, party progression, and economy
[stderr] 
[stderr] **Entities/lifecycle:** `InventoryEntry` is `absent → acquired → usable |
[stderr] equipped → consumed-if-applicable`; `PartyMember` progresses through authored
[stderr] states; `RewardGrant` is `unclaimed → applied`. Inputs are item definitions,
